<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jefferson Davis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jefferson Davis was the only president of the Confederate States of America, from 1861 to 1865. A planter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, he had served as an army officer, senator and US secretary of war. As Confederate president he struggled with states' rights governors and a failing economy, and he was captured in May 1865 and imprisoned for two years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis.jpg" alt="Jefferson Davis" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President-Jefferson-Davis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Confederacy: when Ambrose Dudley Mann reported a letter from Pope Pius IX addressed to &quot;the Illustrious and Honorable <b>Jefferson Davis</b>, President of the Confederate States of America&quot;, Secretary of State Judah P. Benjamin replied that Mann had mistranslated it and that it was &quot;a mere inferential recognition&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>On 20 December 1860 a band played it after each vote for secession in Charleston, South Carolina, and on 18 February 1861 it was played at <b>Jefferson Davis</b>'s inauguration as president of the Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dixie">Dixie</a></li><li><blockquote>As Union troops broke through the Petersburg lines, Confederate President <b>Jefferson Davis</b> and his government fled Richmond by train.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-2">2 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He sat briefly at <b>Jefferson Davis</b>'s desk in the Confederate White House.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He filled the seat <b>Jefferson Davis</b> had left when the South seceded.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Within days they adopted a provisional constitution and chose <b>Jefferson Davis</b> as provisional President.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-4">4 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fairview, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.84,-87.3&amp;year=1860" aria-label="Fairview on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2360 836 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jefferson Davis"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2810 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2810 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.84,-87.3&amp;year=1860">What was here in 1860? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
